# Inequality Mechanisms project rules

- This is scientific research software, not a product prototype.
- Favor correctness, explicitness, and testability over clever abstractions.
- Search state identity lives in input configuration space U.
- Output configuration q and Cartesian state x are attached data, not node identity.
- Preserve duplicate output preimages as distinct physical states.
- Apply shared joint limits in output space Q.
- Do not silently change experiment definitions or mathematical conventions.
- Do not add dependencies without explaining why they are needed.
- Core kinematics and search algorithms must not live only in notebooks.
- Every implementation task must include tests.
- Dijkstra and A* must use documented node-expansion semantics.
- A* optimal cost must be validated against Dijkstra.
- Use NumPy-style docstrings and Python type annotations.
- Prefer small, composable modules.
- Never modify generated results to make an experiment look better.
- Update the relevant ADR when changing a foundational decision.